## Definition
A **Bloom filter** is a small bit array that answers one question: "is this key *possibly* in the set?" It never says "no" for a key that was added, but it sometimes says "maybe" for one that was not.

## Intuition
- Hash each key to a few positions in the array and switch those bits on.
- If any of a key's bits is still off, the key was never added. If all are on, it probably was.
- The win is space: about 10 bits per key gives a false-positive rate near 1%, and the keys themselves are never stored.

## How it works
```graph type=flowchart dir=TB title="Looking up a key"
key [client]: Key
bits [storage]: Bit array
skip [note]: Not present, skip the disk read
disk [datastore]: Read from disk
key -> bits: hash to k positions
bits -> skip: a bit is off
bits -> disk: all bits on
```
1. To add a key, switch on the k bits its hash functions point to.
2. To look a key up, check the same k bits.
3. One bit off means the key is absent. All bits on means it may be present.

## Example
A filter of 10 bits with 2 hash functions, after adding "cat" (bits 1 and 6) and "dog" (bits 3 and 6):

Table: Lookups after adding cat and dog
| Key | Bits checked | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| cat | 1, 6 | maybe present (it was added) |
| emu | 3, 8 | not present, bit 8 is off |
| fox | 1, 3 | maybe present, a false positive |

With m bits, n keys and k hash functions, the chance of a false positive is about:

$$
p \approx \left(1 - e^{-kn/m}\right)^{k}
$$

## Pitfalls and gotchas
- A key cannot be removed: switching its bits off could also remove other keys. A counting Bloom filter keeps a small counter per position instead of a bit.
- The false-positive rate climbs as the filter fills, so size it for the number of keys you expect, not the number you have today.
> [!warning] A "maybe" is not an answer
> Always confirm a "maybe" against the real store. The filter only saves you the lookups that would have failed.

## Related concepts
- Counting Bloom filter
- Cuckoo filter, which supports deletes
- LSM trees, which keep one filter per file to skip disk reads
